Z||000023||Jay||03-11-2001||12:12 PM||||Yes the Allante hubs and the Pontiac 600 spindles are the same dimensions.  Apparently as long as it is a full size axle it will all fit together(with some exeptions, so I was told).  The Allante axles were cut 1/4" to allow for movement in and out, up and down with the suspension.  The spline did not have to be lengthened, there was enough there.  Other than that, I'm using stock Pontiac 6000 vented rotors and calipers.  The chip is stock 94 Cadillac STS as is computer.||||reg||1
Z||000024||Will||03-11-2001||03:40 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jay:<BR><B>Yes the Allante hubs and the Pontiac 600 spindles are the same dimensions.  Apparently as long as it is a full size axle it will all fit together(with some exeptions, so I was told).  The Allante axles were cut 1/4" to allow for movement in and out, up and down with the suspension.  The spline did not have to be lengthened, there was enough there.  Other than that, I'm using stock Pontiac 6000 vented rotors and calipers.  The chip is stock 94 Cadillac STS as is computer.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What are you doing about the inputs from TC, ABS, IPC, Climate control, etc? The Cad shop manual says that without these inputs the stock chip will put the engine into limp-home mode. That's why I suggested the Aurora chip; the Aurora shop manual does not mention limp home mode in connection with these inputs.<BR>||||reg||1
